A(('h2', "2.12 Limitations of the Review"))
A(('p', "This review adopts a focused scoping strategy. It prioritises depth at the intersection of four themes (ODD specification, requirement formalisation, scenario-based testing and quantum optimisation for software testing) over exhaustive coverage of each theme individually, and it is limited to English-language sources published between 2010 and 2026, together with earlier foundational works where they define concepts used in this thesis. Because quantum optimisation for software testing is a rapidly developing field, a small number of recent preprints are included where no peer-reviewed equivalent exists; these are identified as preprints in the reference list, and their findings are interpreted with corresponding caution. Standards and regulations are cited from their official catalogues and texts. Finally, the classification in Table 2.1 and the summaries in Table 2.2 are based on the published descriptions of each study and are therefore bounded by the level of detail those publications report."))
A(('pagebreak',))

# ---------------- Chapter 3 ----------------
A(('h1', "Chapter 3: Methodology"))
A(('h2', "3.1 Research Paradigm and Design"))
A(('p', "The thesis follows design science research: it builds artefacts (the corpus, the extraction method and the QUBO formulation) and evaluates them against defined criteria. The evaluation is experimental and comparative: each artefact is compared with published baselines on fixed metrics, anchored by a case study reconstructed from public records."))

A(('h2', "3.2 Requirements Corpus"))
A(('p', "Phase A is evaluated on real requirements only. The first source is EU Implementing Regulation 2022/1426 {C37}. Its Annex II contains the performance requirements for the automated driving system, including the ODD requirements quoted in Section 2.3; its Annex III contains the assessment provisions. An extraction script recorded 370 points verbatim with their annex and point numbers: all 141 numbered points of Annex II (91 containing \"shall\" or \"must\" and 50 context points such as the list of ODD conditions) and the 229 normative points of Annex III. UN Regulation No. 157 on Automated Lane Keeping Systems {C38} is the second source, and the PURE dataset of 79 public requirements documents {C36} supplies non-automotive requirements for negative examples. Requirements written by the researcher, including those used in the feasibility study, are excluded from evaluation."))
A(('p', "Each requirement is annotated for whether it contains an ODD condition and, if so, its dimension, variable, operator, threshold, unit and required response. Annotation reads each requirement through FRET's scope, condition, timing and response fields {C34}. The dimension schema comprises the ODD conditions enumerated in EU 2022/1426 Annex II point 3.1.4.1, supplemented by speed, traffic participants, temperature and a \"generic\" category for requirements that refer to the ODD as a whole, and its labels are aligned with the taxonomies of ISO 34503 {C6} and PAS 1883 {D66}."))
A(('p', "Two annotators label the corpus independently after a calibration round on 20 requirements. Agreement is reported with Cohen's kappa {COHEN} for the categorical fields and exact-match percentage for variable, operator, threshold and unit. Disagreements are resolved by discussion or by the supervisor as a third annotator. No AI system acts as annotator. The corpus is split 60/20/20 into training, development and test sets, stratified by source and fixed before any model is built, with an additional held-out-source evaluation."))

A(('h2', "3.3 Phase A: Requirement to Traceable ODD Boundary Predicate"))
A(('p', "Phase A follows the architecture of Req2Spec {C19}. Clause-level extraction with ClausIE {D60} isolates the condition clause; a SciBERT-based classifier {D61} assigns the ODD dimension; Sentence-BERT embeddings {D62} match variables to taxonomy entries; and a rule layer extracts operator, threshold and unit. The output for each requirement is a record of the form requirement identifier, ODD dimension, boundary predicate, response, stored so that a change to the requirement can be propagated to every predicate and scenario that depends on it. Where useful, predicates can be exported in the ASAM OpenODD format {C7}."))
A(('p', "Baselines are the rule-based and TF-IDF extractors from the feasibility study (Chapter 4) and zero-shot classification with a large language model. Results are reported as precision, recall and F1 per field on the test split, and compared with Req2Spec's 71% {C19} as a reference point for a related but different task."))

A(('h2', "3.4 Phase B: ODD Predicate to QUBO Scenario Selection"))
A(('p', "Phase B adapts the pipeline of the closest precedent {D17}. First, seed scenarios are generated as logical scenarios {C17} parameterised by the ODD dimensions extracted in Phase A, for example a vehicle approaching a visibility boundary at a given speed with a given pedestrian exposure. Second, selecting scenarios is formulated as a QUBO {D42} with terms for effectiveness, diversity, set size, dimension coverage and a traceability weight that favours scenarios exercising a predicate derived from a stated requirement. Third, large problems are decomposed into sub-problems that fit the hardware, following the decomposition strategies used in BootQA {D19}, IGDec-QAOA {C15} and QAOA-based selection {D18}."))
A(('p', "The QUBO is solved with quantum annealing and with QAOA {D36}. Baselines are random sampling, combinatorial testing, search-based testing {C33}, a greedy coverage heuristic, simulated annealing and NSGA-II, so that results are comparable with both the automated-driving testing literature and the quantum precedent. The precedent's public lab package is used first to reproduce its baseline. Selected scenarios are executed in CARLA {CARLA}, written as Scenic programs {C22}, against a reconstructed fault model of the EA26002 visibility case {D2}."))

A(('h2', "3.5 Evaluation Plan"))
A(('table', "Table 3.1. Research questions, metrics and baselines",
   ["RQ", "Metrics", "Baselines / reference", "Statistical treatment"],
   [["RQ1", "Precision, recall and F1 per annotated field; agreement with gold labels on the test split and held-out source", "Rule-based, TF-IDF and LLM zero-shot extractors; Req2Spec 71% as reference", "95% confidence intervals by bootstrap"],
    ["RQ2", "ODD dimension-combination coverage; input and output diversity; fault detection on the reconstructed fault model", "Random, combinatorial, search-based, greedy, simulated annealing, NSGA-II", "Wilcoxon or Mann-Whitney tests over repeated runs; effect sizes"],
    ["RQ3", "Wall-clock time; QPU access time; embedding time; qubits; problem size at which decomposition is required", "Same as RQ2", "Scaling curves over problem size"],
    ["RQ4", "Share of selected scenarios with an unbroken trace to a source requirement; correctness of change propagation after a requirement edit", "Manual trace audit", "Descriptive, with every broken link listed"]],
   [700, 3200, 3000, 2460]))
A(('p', "**Threats to validity.** The case study is a reconstruction from public information. Simulator results may not hold on noisy hardware. The corpus is drawn from regulations, whose wording may differ from manufacturers' internal requirements. The held-out-source evaluation and the full reporting of all outcomes, including non-significant differences, are the principal mitigations."))
A(('h2', "3.6 Ethics and Research Integrity"))
A(('p', "The second annotator takes part with informed consent, following COMSATS procedures. The case study uses only public regulatory records. All experimental figures are produced by version-controlled code and data that are archived with the thesis. The use of generative AI tools is set out in the Declaration at the front of this thesis, in accordance with university policy; no AI system provides gold-standard labels or evaluation results."))
A(('pagebreak',))

# ---------------- Chapter 4 ----------------
A(('h1', "Chapter 4: Preliminary Feasibility Study"))
A(('p', "Before constructing the corpus and experimental framework described in Chapter 3, I conducted a small-scale feasibility study to establish whether both phases could be realised with openly available tools and to identify design requirements early. The study deliberately used small samples: 15 development and 25 held-out requirement sentences written for the study, and 10 candidate scenarios. Its results are therefore indicative rather than confirmatory; they informed the design decisions in Chapter 3, and the full evaluation on the real-requirements corpus is reported in Chapter 5."))
A(('h2', "4.1 Phase A: Predicate Extraction"))
A(('p', "A rule-based extractor was written from 15 sentences and scored on the same sentences: 93.3% correct ODD dimension (14 of 15) and 80.0% fully correct including the predicate (12 of 15). On 25 new sentences scored without adjustment, dimension accuracy fell to 32.0% and full correctness to 28.0%. A TF-IDF similarity classifier built from the 15 training sentences scored 28.0% on the same 25. Zero-shot classification of the 25 sentences by a large language model assigned the dimension correctly in 24 cases (96.0%); the one disagreement concerned a daylight-only restriction that could be read as a lighting or a time condition."))
A(('table', "Table 4.1. Phase A feasibility-study results",
   ["Method", "Evaluation set", "Result", "Limitation"],
   [["Rule-based", "15 sentences it was written from", "93.3% dimension; 80.0% full", "Fit to its own sentences"],
    ["Rule-based", "25 unseen sentences", "32.0% dimension; 28.0% full", "Small sample"],
    ["TF-IDF similarity", "25 unseen sentences", "28.0%", "15 training sentences across 9 categories"],
    ["LLM zero-shot", "25 unseen sentences", "96.0% dimension (24 of 25)", "Single annotator; dimension only, not thresholds"]],
   [1800, 2600, 2400, 2560]))
A(('p', "Two findings shaped the method adopted in Chapter 3. First, the drop from 80% to 28% shows that rules fitted to a handful of sentences do not generalise, which motivates a trained pipeline and a real corpus. Second, the LLM result cannot serve as evidence of accuracy, because the gold labels were written by a single person and the task covered only the dimension; Chapter 3 therefore uses two independent human annotators and restricts LLMs to a baseline role."))
A(('h2', "4.2 Phase B: QUBO Scenario Selection"))
A(('p', "Ten candidate scenarios covering six ODD sub-dimensions were formulated as a QUBO with terms for effectiveness, pairwise diversity and a target of four scenarios, and solved by random sampling (200 trials), a greedy coverage heuristic, simulated annealing and a one-layer QAOA circuit with fixed parameters on a statevector simulator. Without a coverage term, random search, simulated annealing and QAOA converged to the same solution (objective -15.33), covering four of six dimensions, while greedy covered all six with a worse objective (-11.75). The objective did not reward coverage. With a coverage bonus and a redundancy penalty, simulated annealing and QAOA covered five of six dimensions and greedy six of six; QAOA's untuned parameters selected six scenarios instead of four. Variational optimisation of the QAOA parameters was outside the scope of this study and forms part of Phase B."))
A(('table', "Table 4.2. Phase B feasibility-study results (10 scenarios, simulators)",
   ["Method", "Dimensions covered (no coverage term)", "Dimensions covered (with coverage term)"],
   [["Random sampling (200 trials)", "4 of 6", "4 of 6"],
    ["Greedy coverage heuristic", "6 of 6", "6 of 6"],
    ["Simulated annealing", "4 of 6", "5 of 6"],
    ["QAOA, 1 layer, fixed parameters", "4 of 6", "5 of 6 (selected 6 scenarios, target 4)"]],
   [3400, 2980, 2980]))
A(('p', "The feasibility study supports no claim of quantum advantage. It shows that the formulation must reward coverage explicitly and that QAOA parameters must be tuned, both of which are built into Phase B."))

